    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110050\">@Sébastien Gouëzel</span> the oops doesn't seems wrong? foreach n(here n = 0) have the same k. Thus, k must be equal. I just want to prove unreachability, thus the False proof?</p>\n<p>Allowed is just there to model one of the hidden constructor parameters <code>Allowed .fst n</code> is impossible to construct because inside the case h.snd all <code>Allowed k n</code> must be <code>Allowed .snd n</code>. Thus, it is impossible to construct <code>Goal .snd n</code> inside that branch because you need <code>Allowed .fst n</code>. I was thinking of using <code>K.fst = K.snd</code> to prove unreachability. But I can't for the love of god, figure out how to get <code>K.fst = K.snd</code>it cleanly without any \"hacks\".</p>\n<p>It seems like the only way to prove unreachability is from hypothesis in lean.</p>",
        "id": 592072619,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777559512
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>you could prove <code>oops</code>, then use <code>False.elim oops</code>?</p>",
        "id": 592073457,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777559698
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Unless I'm misunderstanding, <code>same_k_foreach_n</code> is capable of proving 0 ≠ 0, and if that doesn't seem wrong then you should take advantage of it</p>",
        "id": 592074131,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777559851
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay there is a hole there. I guess I can prove everything with <code>oops</code> then. But, let say you can't use it for now. Is there a way to pick out the required parameter <code>Allowed .fst n</code>from Goal and then use same_k_foreach_n to get <code>K.fst = K.snd</code> and from there prove unreachability?</p>",
        "id": 592076071,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777560319
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Does the <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/Proving.20subgoal.20unreachable.20by.20parameter.20incompatibility/near/592067544\">theorem <code>allow</code></a> also need to be ruled out? It would let you do <code>intro n h ; exact Goal.fst n (allow K.fst n)</code></p>",
        "id": 592080238,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777561252
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In other words, perhaps we should take Sébastien's concern more seriously</p>",
        "id": 592080395,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777561286
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What I'm saying is just that your definition of <code>Allowed</code> is not correct. It says that everyone is allowed.</p>",
        "id": 592080718,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1777561366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's possible you're intending to treat <code>Allowed</code> as an unknown, instead of something with two big constructors. Eg like <code>def Allowed : K → Nat → Prop := ...</code></p>",
        "id": 592082956,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777561866
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think to plug the hole, I need to plug everything with k and n with a proof of k &lt;-&gt; n.</p>\n<p>Is there a way for you to answer my main question? Whether you can carve out <code>Allowed .fst n</code> from the Goal parameter and prove unreachability? Or does it need to be proven by the hypothesis always? Lean doesn't seem to allow me carving out that parameter atm.</p>",
        "id": 592093536,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777564297
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>since you can already prove <code>False</code> with the axiom <code>same_k_foreach_n</code>, there's nothing interesting that can be done, since every goal can be filled with a <code>False.elim</code></p>",
        "id": 592094988,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1777564651
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>More importantly, it's unclear what your main question is. Could you describe how \"Allowed\" is supposed to work, eg the valid ways of creating an Allowed?</p>",
        "id": 592104716,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777566548
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The question of what kinds of proof are possible depends heavily on what Allowed is, or what it's intended to be</p>",
        "id": 592105010,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777566623
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For example, is \"Allowed\" meant to describe a <code>Nat → K</code> function? or a <code>Nat → K → Prop</code> function? Or a data structure that contains a <code>Nat</code> and a <code>K</code> (which is essentially the current code)?</p>",
        "id": 592105669,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777566788
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or something else?</p>",
        "id": 592105795,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777566819
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Allowed is supposed to be K -&gt; Nat -&gt; Prop. In my real example it is a bit more complex than that. It is more like an id to a certain unique set. Where each set depending on certain properties of the set it uses different constructors. But once constructed that id cannot be claimed by any set anymore.</p>",
        "id": 592125795,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777572307
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Though. I think I’m at a point where I can solve my problem. But please correct me if I’m wrong. Lean doesn’t allow disproving goal arguments to prove false?</p>\n<p>They can only be proven false by hypothesis. Thus I must deconstruct arguments to hypothesis first. I must prove which k are disallowed first. Thus, in this case I must prove ’k&lt;-&gt; allowed arguments k in Goal’. From there I can prove unreachability with same theorem as above but switch out goal with ”allowed arguments k in Goal”. But then we can map ’ allowed arguments k in Goal -&gt; Goal k’ to get the original theorem.</p>",
        "id": 592131962,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777573932
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If I'm reading correctly, proving unreachable <em>should</em> be possible. Eg the following argument can be translated into Lean:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Theorem: suppose A ∨ B. Then ¬A → B.<br>\nProof: Intro ¬A.<br>\nWe argue by cases on A ∨ B.<br>\n- Case 1: A. This is absurd, because ¬A.<br>\n- Case 2: B. By assumption.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 592133370,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777574365
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But be careful about the phrasing of the theorem, too.<br>\nThe signature <code>(n : Nat) -&gt; (h : Allowed k n) -&gt; Goal K.snd n</code> says, <em>\"if Allowed k n, then Goal K.snd n, no matter what k and n are.\"</em> Nothing prevents Allowed K.snd n.</p>",
        "id": 592134845,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777574829
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's quite literally a function. \"Given an <code>n</code> and the fact <code>Allowed k n</code>, returns the fact <code>Goal K.snd n</code>.\" If someone calls the function when k = K.snd, that branch will get reached</p>",
        "id": 592135310,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777574980
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe I use the terms wrong here. But hypothesis is ’not A’ in not A implies B in your example. But also an argument of course. But I was referring to constructor arguments. If a goal is an inductive type you cannot reach unreachability with the data from the goal?</p>",
        "id": 592136206,
        "sender_full_name": "Tingan Ho",
        "timestamp": 1777575261
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps the goal is the wrong way round?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Goal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Allowed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Goal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>means \"there is exactly one way of making <code>Goal k n</code>. If you see <code>Goal k n</code>, then there must be <code>Allowed K.fst n</code>.\"<br>\nbut if you see <code>Allowed K.snd n</code>, that tells you that you <em>can't</em> make <code>Goal k n</code>. This is the opposite of what your example tries to prove</p>",
